			<div class="content" id="post-content-2277729">
									

<p>Brief run-through:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>consistent with an anomalously sharp kitchen knife</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The question is: What constitutes an &quot;anomalously sharp kitchen knife&quot;? Your elaboration of it seems like a stronger version of a knife (that could cut through steel). It might do well to have a clearer (and empirically defined) description of the knife's sharpness.</p>
<p>On that note, what would an invisible kitchen knife need to be <em>so</em> sharp?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Researcher Clarent's work obligations - as tested by Clarent by swinging at the air around her, saying &quot;It was worth a try.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Is this <em>really</em> necessary? It's borderline unprofessional behaviour and there is no indication that 2124-1 can &quot;cut&quot; through abstract concepts (That could be a cool concept to work with though).</p>
<p>Even so, how would cutting the air around oneself cut an abstract concept tied to oneself? It is slightly more logically to be cutting yourself or your brain or something&#8230; but I digress.</p>
<p>Pros:</p>
<ul>
<li>Admittedly, this is the first time I see an imaginary knife drawn from a pizza box as though it were a sheath.</li>
</ul>
<p>Cons:</p>
<ul>
<li>The &quot;how it was found&quot; story felt <em>way too</em> convenient. Unless I am mistaken, a researcher's friend just messaged him that he found a potential SCP by chance. This implies unprofessionalism on part of the researcher (a major security compromise) and a level of coincidence that is jarring to believe in. A stronger origin may have been a random civilian accidentally using the SCP. While relatively mundane (and even cliche), it is easier to believe than a researcher's friend calling him about a possible anomalous item.</li>
<li>In the main bulk of the article, you never explicitly explained what SCP-2124 is exactly. I can infer that it is an invisible knife, but it took a while to realise that. It might do well to state SCP-2124-1's existence and what it is, before jumping to casually talking about it and its properties.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>

